From f7ce7b56b5f0c26051c5a010e6f7421c2c6ee292 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Bruno=20Greg=C3=B3rio?= <accounts@bruno.pw>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 15:37:45 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] ipa: simple: Add a contrast detection autofocus
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Cameras with a focus lens on the simple pipeline stay at whatever focus
the lens rests at. Using the focus statistics, scan the lens from
infinity towards macro in coarse steps, stopping once the sharpness has
clearly fallen past a peak, then step finely around the best coarse
position and settle on the best fine one, refined by a parabola through
it and its neighbours. A scan whose best is not clearly above its worst
fails and leaves the lens at the default position.

Noise adds to the sharpness and grows with the gain, so a scan waits
for the AGC to stop changing the exposure and gain and for the
brightness to hold still, and starts over if the brightness moves away
during it. A scene too dark to show anything is not scanned.

AfMode, AfTrigger, AfPause and LensPosition work as the control
definitions describe. In continuous mode the algorithm keeps the
sharpness and brightness it focused at and scans again once either has
changed for a while and the scene has then steadied.

The tuning file chooses the default mode, manual unless it says
otherwise, as an application reaching the camera through PipeWire has
no way to set it. It can also give the lens positions of infinity and
of the closest focus, and how near that is in dioptres, which
LensPosition is reckoned in; without them the lens's whole range stands
for infinity to 10 cm.

Signed-off-by: Bruno Gregório <accounts@bruno.pw>
